<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document><name>ILLUSTRATIVE ariane64-le04-2026-11-23 ascent (3DOF sim)</name><description>ILLUSTRATIVE ASCENT ECEF — Ariane 64 Block 2 VA271 / Amazon Leo LE-04 (ELA-4) — NOT OFFICIAL TELEMETRY
Method: first-principles 3DOF sim (/workspace/trajectory-deck/sim/ascent3dof.py): WGS-84 rotating Earth, J2, US76 drag,
TIMELINE mode: every ignition/cutoff/separation/fairing time is a published hard event; mdot=F_vac/(Isp_vac*g0); thrust SL/vac on ambient pressure.
FITTED (least squares vs published knots): vertical rise 7.37 s, kick 8.155 deg over 10 s, Cd 0.400, payload 20855 kg,
gravity turn, then linear-tangent steering tan(theta)=1.8749-0.002970*(t-t_ign stage 2).
Vehicle config: Ariane 64 Block 2 (4x P160C, Vulcain 2.1, Vinci) — TIMELINE mode (sim/vehicles/ariane64_block2.json; every number carries a source URL, derivation, or ASSUMPTION).
Ariane 64 Block 2. P160C propellant, gross mass, Isp and 137 s burn from Wikipedia P160C. Constant thrust is the average that consumes that load, not the 4,780 kN maximum.
LLPM propellant 150 t from CNES (Wikipedia 140 t does not close with 1,370 kN at 432 s over a 465 s MECO). Dry mass 23 t from ESA. Vulcain vacuum thrust 1,370 kN (Wikipedia) and 432 s (eoPortal). Sea-level thrust 135 tonnes-force (CNES).
ULPM 30 t / Vinci 180 kN / 457.2 s. Dry mass 6 t from Wikipedia. Fairing 2.6 t is an ASSUMPTION inside Beyond Gravity&#x27;s 1.8-2.6 t range for the 20 m shell.
TIMELINE mode: ignition, cutoff, separation and fairing times are hard events. mdot = F_vac/(Isp_vac*g0). Full thrust; throttle unpublished.
ILLUSTRATIVE. PRE-FLIGHT. Ariane 64 Block 2, flight VA271, Amazon Leo LE-04, 36 satellites, ELA-4. T0 is the window open 2026-11-23T11:09:00Z (t0_kind window_open), window to 11:38:00Z. Arianespace: 11:09-11:38 UTC, which is 12:09-12:38 Paris. LL2 id 7bdc412b-c965-4517-a131-f57a01189803 is Go, net 2026-11-23T11:09:00Z, window 11:09-11:38Z, last_updated 2026-11-20T14:23:25Z.
TIME CONFLICT: ESA&#x27;s VA271 timeline labels its clock column &#x27;CET&#x27; but the liftoff row 11:09:22 matches UTC, not Paris. Arianespace lists 12:09-12:38 Paris and 11:09-11:38 UTC. ESA also writes the window as 11:09-11:38 GMT / 12:09-12:38 CET, so the timeline&#x27;s 11:09:22 liftoff is the UTC/GMT figure with a CET label. Both are recorded. This file uses 11:09:00Z, the window open, not ESA&#x27;s 11:09:22.
Published timeline (ESA, offsets from that liftoff): booster sep T+2:23, fairing sep T+3:12, core MECO T+7:45, core sep T+7:51, Vinci ignition 1 T+7:59, Vinci cutoff 1 T+17:51, Vinci ignition 2 T+1:06:52, Vinci cutoff 2 T+1:07:10, deployment starts T+1:39:29, ends T+2:08:39.
Azimuth 37.23 deg from NAVAREA IV 1118/26 areas A and B. The Vinci-cutoff-1 fit target (geodetic 150 km, apogee parameter 445 km, FPA -0.55 deg) is an ASSUMPTION chosen so the open-loop 18 s Vinci burn finishes near the borrowed 465 km. Payload 20,855 kg is a provisional estimate. ILLUSTRATIVE.
Target (see launch config): insertion 150 km, apoapsis 445 km, fpa 0, launch azimuth 37.23 deg; payload 20855 kg.
Pad: 5.256319N -52.786838E. Frame: WGS-84 ECEF, positions km, velocities km/s (Earth-relative).
T0 used: 2026-11-23T11:09:00Z. Re-run with another T0 by shifting utc_iso only; ECEF geometry is Earth-fixed.
POST-CUTOFF: after final cutoff T+1071.0 s the stage coasts (same WGS-84/J2/US76-drag model) to PAYLOAD DEPLOY T+5969 s; rows tagged -coast/-burn.
POST-CUTOFF burn &#x27;Vinci boost 2&#x27;: ign T+4012.0 s, 18.0 s at 180.00 kN prograde (Isp 457.2 s); ESA VA271 published second-burn timing, rated Vinci thrust and Isp, open-loop. Not solved to a target perigee. The APU (CNES slide: about 250 N) is not modelled.
POST-CUTOFF deploy T+5969 s (ESA VA271 timeline: deployment starts T+1:39:29 (5969 s). Deployment ends T+2:08:39 (7719 s); the file ends at the start.): alt 488.4 km, osculating 463.9 x 489.1 km, i 51.122 deg.
POST-CUTOFF note: Deployment ends at T+2:08:39 (7719 s) per the same ESA timeline. This file ends when deployment starts.
POST-CUTOFF note: ESA labels the timeline column CET, but 11:09:22 matches the UTC/GMT liftoff (Arianespace: 12:09 Paris). Offsets are counted from that liftoff row.
POST-CUTOFF note: No deorbit burn is published on this timeline (LE-01 had a third burn near T+2:42). It is not modelled.</description>
